# Investigating a Suspicious deployment in Azure.

## Scenario
Intern with temporary contributer access deployed a test enviornment without following the company governance standards. The investigation revealed what the intern had deployed in the subscription.


## Environment
Live multi-user Azure training tenant, Reader access

## Investigation
1) Looked at the list of Resource Groups, there was one resource group that didn't follow the standard naming convention.

![Alt text](https://github.com/jts-cloud/security-portfolio/blob/d2b38f258239274b3ec5a0e9cdb5d9c7b6606120/Images/Resource%20Manager%20Blade.png)

2) In the Overview Blade of the outlier Resource Group, there was only one resource listed. 



3) Looked at tags in the tag blade of the single resource and found an Owner tag that identified the intern as the owner of the resource.

4) Found a single deployment in the Deployments Blade for the outlier resource group.

5) Moved over to the outlier resource group's policy page and found two Non-compliant policies

6) Clicked on the Naming Convention Policy and saw under Details that the Effect type was set to Audit. 

## What broke / what surprised me
I found it surprising just how easy it is for things to go south if your policies are misconfigured or not configured the way you would like them to behave. 

## Findings and recommendations
There was a naming convention policy in place that had the Effect type "Audit". My recommendation would be to change this policy to Effect type deny. I would also recommend a review of all policies to ensure they are
set to the desired configurations. 

## What I learned
* It is highly important to stick to a naming convention due to how many properties can be packed into a single subscription, uninformative naming conventions can cost your sanity and time when trying to investigate.
* There are numerous ways of starting and performing an investigation. There isn't one correct way. 
* Tags are insanely useful and can provide you with a wealth of knowledge. The inclusion (or lack of certain ones) can help narrow down where you should be looking in an investigation. 
